    "body": "A spectrogram is a picture of sound with time running left to right, frequency running bottom to top, and loudness shown by darkness or colour. It is computed by slicing a recording into short overlapping windows and measuring the frequency content of each window, a procedure called the short-time Fourier transform. Spectrograms let researchers see what ears miss: the rising sweep of a bat call, the harmonic stack of a bird song, the repeated pulses of a frog call, and the separate frequency bands used by different species in a chorus. Reading a spectrogram takes care: window length trades time resolution against frequency resolution, so a short window pinpoints when a sound happened but blurs its pitch, and a long window does the opposite. Spectrograms display all three layers at once, biophony, geophony, and anthrophony, which is why analysts learn to recognise each layer's visual signature.",
